, 'quoted_status_result': {'result': {'__typename': 'Tweet', 'rest_id': '1848451846203183455', 'core': {'user_results': {'result': {'__typename': 'User', 'id': 'VXNlcjoxNjc0Nzc1MTYyNjQwNzkzNjAw', 'rest_id': '1674775162640793600', 'affiliates_highlighted_label': {}, 'has_graduated_access': True, 'is_blue_verified': True, 'profile_image_shape': 'Circle', 'legacy': {'can_dm': False, 'can_media_tag': True, 'created_at': 'Fri Jun 30 13:42:53 +0000 2023', 'default_profile': True, 'default_profile_image': False, 'description': 'Retired Chief Architect. Musk’s Vision+Skills Unique. Tesla Investor & MY Owner. Healthy Living. Old Man Wisdom. Tesla referral link: https://t.co/w3gFEZnXHh', 'entities': {'description': {'urls': [{'display_url': 'ts.la/barry537225', 'expanded_url': 'https://ts.la/barry537225', 'url': 'https://t.co/w3gFEZnXHh', 'indices': [134, 157]}]}}, 'fast_followers_count': 0, 'favourites_count': 3626, 'followers_count': 3232, 'friends_count': 171, 'has_custom_timelines': False, 'is_translator': False, 'listed_count': 13, 'location': '', 'media_count': 3574, 'name': 'Barry Schiff', 'normal_followers_count': 3232, 'pinned_tweet_ids_str': ['1954465088376852492'], 'possibly_sensitive': False, 'profile_banner_url': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_banners/1674775162640793600/1691756929', 'profile_image_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1689974582592393216/pj6JFk8w_normal.jpg', 'profile_interstitial_type': '', 'screen_name': 'KissIsTheKey', 'statuses_count': 27267, 'translator_type': 'none', 'verified': False, 'want_retweets': False, 'withheld_in_countries': []}}}}, 'unmention_data': {}, 'edit_control': {'edit_tweet_ids': ['1848451846203183455'], 'editable_until_msecs': '1729543837000', 'is_edit_eligible': False, 'edits_remaining': '5'}, 'views': {'count': '19405', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="https://mobile.twitter.com" rel="nofollow">Twitter Web App</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4NDg0NTE4NDUwODc0OTgyNDM=', 'text': 'Unhealthy Americans\nLove it – Vote Harris, Hate it – Vote Trump\nWe pay the most per capita in the world for our healthcare yet have the least to show for it. Out of approximately 200 countries ranked for life expectancy, we used to be among the best; now, we rank 48th, the lowest we\'ve ever been, with a trend that\'s not in our favor. So, what went wrong over the decades, and how do we fix it?\n\nWHAT WE\'RE DOING WRONG:\n\nHighly Processed Foods\nWe\'ve shifted away from diverse, small, family-run farms and ranches to a world dominated by big agriculture. Over many decades, our farming, ranching, and food supply have become more like science experiments rather than focusing on care for the land, soil, and quality of the food production. Corporate interests now aim to extract every possible short-term profit.\n\nDid you know out of the 10,000+ chemicals and bio-engineered fertilizers, pesticides, seeds, and additives used in U.S. food production almost all are banned in Europe and many other parts of the world? \n\nThe U.S. regulatory approach tends to permits these substances unless they\'re proven harmful, whereas Europe requires them to be proven safe before use, leading to a much stricter and smaller list of unnatural substances present in their food.\n\nDid you know that the U.S. government pressures countries or regions that ban Genetically Modified Organisms (GMOs) to accept them? \n\nDid you know companies like Monsanto engineer their GMO seeds to only yield for one season, forcing farmers to buy seeds annually? Yes, you heard correctly; they intentionally engineer seeds to be non-reproductive. \n\nI could share many more stories that would make your hair stand on end, but you\'re likely getting the picture: we\'re far down the wrong path in terms of health, self-reliance, environmental stewardship, and longevity.\n\nIn the U.S., we consume more of our daily calories from highly processed foods than any other country. Coincidentally, we also lead the world in obesity rates and are about to fall out of the top 25% for life expectancy.\n\nThe general rule is: the more processed a food, the worse it is for you. Organic standards, which big agriculture has tried to dilute in the USA, offer a solid foundation. \n\nEncouraging the production and consumption of whole, clean, minimally processed foods is a clear step toward improving our health.\n\nBig Agriculture Industry\'s Dominance:\nWe need to break big agriculture\'s monopoly on our food supply and shift back to thousands of small farms and ranches. We should incentivize organic, wholesome, minimally processed foods and sustainable farming practices. Our food supply should not be controlled by a handful of powerful companies.\n\nLess Exercise\nIn the 1960s, when I started school, all the kids were extremely active and fit. Maybe a handful of children in the entire school were obese, and those unfortunate individuals really stood out and were the target of lots of ridicule.\n\nWe all played outside all day every day. This was before cable TV, mobile phones, and the internet. Moms would wave us goodbye daily saying, “Make sure you\'re home by dinner,” and off we all went on our outdoor, physically active journeys.\n\nAt school, we had daily gym classes. Back then, there was a significant societal focus on physical fitness, encouraged by national initiatives like the Presidential Physical Fitness Award. All the heroes of the day were in great shape. Being physically fit was expected and admired.\n\nConversely, we considered non-fit people to be unattractive. Was that fair to the rare group of people who actually had a medical condition that caused them to be obese no matter how hard they tried to be fit? Of course not, but was society healthier with its intense focus and love for physical fitness? Of course, it was.\n\nIt\'s a far cry from where we are today. Today\'s lack of physical activity in the average American family is deteriorating our country\'s health, happiness, and wellbeing. \n\nBig Pharmaceutical Companies\' Excessive Influence\nMany doctors receive only a brief introduction to nutrition, often within a single semester course during their medical training. This training heavily emphasizes procedures and treatments which are either directly or indirectly sponsored by large pharmaceutical companies, occasionally supplemented with funding from major agricultural firms.\n\nI do not criticize our doctors; they are diligent, highly intelligent individuals who have navigated vast amounts of educational material, dedicating countless hours and making numerous personal sacrifices to reach their current positions. They strive to do the right thing and genuinely aim to assist the public.\n\nHowever, I question whether these professionals are adequately equipped, incentivized, and empowered to serve their patients to the best of their abilities. \n\nAre we providing them with a comprehensive set of relevant information, along with financial and organizational incentives, and the autonomy to truly optimize patient outcomes?\n\nFrom my perspective as a consumer, the current system prioritizes the profitability of pharmaceutical companies over patient health outcomes. Here are a few illustrative points:\n\nInfluence on Mass Media Outlets\nThe United States and New Zealand are the only countries where pharmaceutical companies can advertise prescription medications directly to consumers on television. \n\nI believe pharmaceutical companies are not truly targeting consumers with their ads; but rather are targeting to influence media companies.\n\nHow often do we see negative stories about pharmaceutical products on TV news? Not frequently, and when they do appear, they are usually brief and superficial. This contrasts sharply with the narratives that media outlets choose to emphasize repeatedly.\n\nThis scenario presents a clear conflict of interest: media outlets, meant to inform the public, are heavily influenced by their biggest sponsors, who could be implicated in some of the world\'s most pressing health issues.\n\nInfluence on the Education of Medical Professionals\n> Funding and Sponsorships: Pharmaceutical companies frequently fund research, provide educational grants, and sponsor conferences, which can bias content towards their products.\n\n> Gifts and Perks:  Companies offer free drug samples, meals, and travel, fostering a favorable perception of their products.\n\n> Educational Materials: Textbooks, software, and online tools often subtly incorporate company products into their content.\n\n> Lectures and Symposia: Industry representatives, sometimes presented as independent experts, may give talks promoting new drugs or treatments.\n\n> Continuing Medical Education (CME): CME courses, essential for maintaining licenses, are often influenced or sponsored by pharmaceutical companies.\n\n> Clinical Trials:  The design, funding, and reporting of clinical trials can be swayed to favor the sponsoring company\'s products and denigrate alternative treatments.\n\n> Curriculum Influence: The pervasive industry presence can skew medical education away from alternative treatments or critical pharmaceutical analysis.\n\n> Regulatory and Policy Influence: Pharmaceutical sector outspends all others in lobbying the U.S. government, and who knows how much undisclosed amounts are possibly spent. \n\nGovernment oversight bodies often share royalties from new drugs developed by the companies they are supposed to regulate. Personnel from these agencies frequently transition into high-ranking positions in the industries they once oversaw, creating a cozy relationship.\n\nThis setup raises serious questions about the government\'s ability to serve public health needs over the interests of financially potent corporations.\n\nCapitalism vs. Socialized Medicine Debate\nSome have questioned whether capitalism is the best approach to healthcare. My perspective is that the USA\'s track record with cost-efficient, government-run (socialist) services is, in a word, horrendous. \n\nGiven this, entrusting the government with one of the country\'s most critical life-and-death services—healthcare—would be extremely unwise.\n\nHowever, this doesn\'t mean I advocate for laissez-faire policies in the medical industry either. I firmly believe it\'s essential for the government to establish a robust framework of laws and incentives to ensure that health services are diverse, continuously improving, and focused on real, cost-effective patient outcomes rather than boosting big company profits.\n\nCurrently, we do not have socialized medicine (where the government controls doctors and hospitals), which, in my opinion, would be disastrous in the USA. \n\nBut we also lack a solid government framework that genuinely directs the industry to serve the best interests of the people. Consequently, we find ourselves in a no-man\'s-land where large corporations benefit and every else pays the price. \n\nInsufficient Data Available to Consumers and Policy Makers\nYou can\'t improve something if you don\'t measure it. People who successfully manage large, complex organizations know this. Without the right measurements, you are essentially flying blind. \n\nThe devil is in the details, and selecting the appropriate metrics while ensuring their accuracy is challenging, but we urgently need to pursue this path.\n\nConsumers cannot make informed decisions without access to accurate, relevant data. These metrics must be easy for the public to understand and objective. Fundamentally, data such as cost, success rates, and impact on life expectancy are essential. \n\nMetrics like these will enable people to evaluate treatment options, including the same treatment provided by different suppliers, equipping them with invaluable information. \n\nI believe there is a role for the government in defining these metrics, guaranteeing data integrity, and ensuring that this information is compiled and readily accessible to both consumers and policymakers.\n\nDIFFERENT APPROACHES TO THE PROBLEM\n\nHarris Approach\nEssentially, expect more of the same, and perhaps even worse. Nobody would be surprised if Harris moved towards socialized medical coverage in the US, similar to what they have in the UK, for example. So, if you want your healthcare provider to be run as efficiently as your local Department of Motor Vehicles, then she\'s your candidate. \n\nEven if she merely expands and continues the current system without any fundamental changes, we\'ll stay on the same trajectory of sliding off a cliff, paying more and getting less, as explained throughout this post. So, the status quo is really, really bad. In fact, it might kill you.\n\nKennedy (As Part of Trump Team) Approach\nRobert F. Kennedy Jr. has decided to align with the Trump team following significant barriers in his bid to compete in the Democratic primary, which were erected by the Democratic Party itself. \n\nHis subsequent attempt to run as an independent was also impeded by  "lawfare" from the Democratic Party. \n\nThis series of events placed Kennedy in a challenging position. Now at 70, though he remains in excellent physical condition, he feels a pressing urgency to reform the healthcare industry now while he is still physically and mentally able to. \n\nKennedy, a lifelong Democrat, found common ground with the Trump administration\'s objectives in several key areas: health, free speech, and the integrity of our voting system. \n\nThis alignment was sufficient for him to switch allegiances, especially after negotiating with President Trump to lead the initiative to "Make America Healthy Again" (MAHA), despite retaining differing views on some other matters.\n\nIn discussions, Kennedy articulately addresses the flaws in the current healthcare system, drawing from his unique perspective shaped by his upbringing in the Kennedy family, which gave him firsthand insights into government operations and corruption. \n\nHis career as a lawyer gave him many decades of experience confronting major corporations in the agriculture and pharmaceutical industries. In doing so he has become imminently familiar with their shortcomings as it applies the health and wellbeing of Americans. \n\nHis journey, marked by legal battles and advocacy, underscores his deep commitment to transforming American healthcare for the better.\n\nWe need a major refactoring of our healthcare and agriculture industries, along with their surrounding regulations, processes, and incentives.\n\nRobert F. Kennedy has a very specific plan already defined; he knows where the skeletons are and is familiar with many of the players. \n\nHe is uniquely qualified to bring real change that will lead to meaningful improvements in our health. We are very fortunate to have this opportunity.\n\nDo you want our country to continue paying more, dying earlier and experiencing more chronic illnesses than ever before?\n\nDon’t be stupid;\nDon’t vote for even more Unhealthy Americans.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': [{'from_index': 0, 'to_index': 19, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 20, 'to_index': 63, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 397, 'to_index': 420, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 422, 'to_index': 444, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 2362, 'to_index': 2399, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 2700, 'to_index': 2713, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 3971, 'to_index': 4020, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 5197, 'to_index': 5228, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 6014, 'to_index': 6065, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 6066, 'to_index': 6093, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 6246, 'to_index': 6265, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 6373, 'to_index': 6397, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 6499, 'to_index': 6523, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 6645, 'to_index': 6683, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 6796, 'to_index': 6814, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 6966, 'to_index': 6990, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 7120, 'to_index': 7155, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 7719, 'to_index': 7760, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 8876, 'to_index': 8934, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 9919, 'to_index': 9954, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 9956, 'to_index': 9971, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 10599, 'to_index': 10639, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 12254, 'to_index': 12396, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 12687, 'to_index': 12807, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 12809, 'to_index': 12825, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}, {'from_index': 12826, 'to_index': 12871, 'richtext_types': ['Bold']}]}, 'media': {'inline_media': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 11, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Mon Oct 21 19:50:37 +0000 2024', 'conversation_id_str': '1848451846203183455', 'display_text_range': [0, 280], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'media': [{'display_url': 'pic.x.com/X2bDVem2ld', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/KissIsTheKey/status/1848451846203183455/photo/1', 'id_str': '1848437997487714305', 'indices': [281, 304], 'media_key': '3_1848437997487714305', 'media_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/media/Gab4kLWW8AEb-_c.jpg', 'type': 'photo', 'url': 'https://t.co/X2bDVem2ld', 'ext_media_availability': {'status': 'Available'}, 'features': {'large': {'faces': [{'x': 831, 'y': 465, 'h': 208, 'w': 208}]}, 'medium': {'faces': [{'x': 824, 'y': 461, 'h': 206, 'w': 206}]}, 'small': {'faces': [{'x': 467, 'y': 261, 'h': 116, 'w': 116}]}, 'orig': {'faces': [{'x': 831, 'y': 465, 'h': 208, 'w': 208}]}}, 'sizes': {'large': {'h': 824, 'w': 1209, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'medium': {'h': 818, 'w': 1200, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'small': {'